Metaphorically Animatronic

A Satirical Study of Institutional Adults Repeating Instructions from an Economy That No Longer Exists

Abstract

A recurring puzzle occupies contemporary political and economic discourse: why are young adults insufficiently enthusiastic?

They appear reluctant to work themselves to death, nervous about the future, pessimistic about housing, concerned about climate change, increasingly sceptical of institutions and occasionally dependent upon pharmaceutical assistance merely to tolerate the experience.

The usual explanation is some variant of personal deficiency.

They lack resilience.
They have been mollycoddled.
They expect too much.
They do not want to work.
They have been taught to see themselves as victims.

This paper investigates an alternative hypothesis:

Have we considered the possibility that they have been listening?

The present generation of young adults has entered an economy in which housing is dramatically less affordable than it was for previous entrants, while some of the wealthiest and most technologically influential adults on Earth simultaneously advertise a future in which substantial categories of human employment may disappear.

They have also inherited an environmental system already substantially altered by previous human activity and are periodically informed that psychological distress arising within this environment is itself evidence of defective character.

The paper proposes the Metaphorically Animatronic Hypothesis: that significant parts of the adult political and economic establishment have become functionally comparable to ageing theme-park machinery, capable of repeating inherited instructions — work hard, buy a house, build a career, stop complaining — despite the physical environment around those instructions having changed beyond recognition.

The resulting question is not why young adults have failed to become sufficiently optimistic.

It is:

What the fuck, precisely, were we expecting them to be optimistic about?

