1. Introduction: The Young Person Has Failed to Perform Correctly

The modern young adult is an interesting experimental subject.

At approximately eighteen years of age, the specimen is released into the economy.

It is then given several instructions.

Obtain employment.

Develop a career.

Become financially independent.

Save money.

Purchase property.

Prepare for retirement.

Start a family if desired.

Remain adaptable because the jobs may disappear.

Acquire qualifications for those jobs anyway.

Accept that artificial intelligence may fundamentally restructure the labour market.

Continue contributing to an economy whose long-term environmental consequences previous generations have largely handed down to you.

Remain psychologically well throughout this process.

Should the specimen display anxiety, depression, anger or reluctance, the experimenter records:

Subject exhibits poor resilience.

The possibility that the experiment itself is fucking ridiculous is rarely entered into the laboratory notes.

This paper therefore studies what will be called the Animatronic Establishment.

This is not an age cohort.

Nigel Farage, Elon Musk and Sam Altman are not members of the same generation. That is precisely the point. The phenomenon crosses generations.

The defining characteristic is not date of birth but position.

The Animatronic Establishment consists of sufficiently secure adults who retain access to economic, political or cultural power while continuing to issue behavioural instructions formed under conditions increasingly unavailable to the people receiving them.

Its defining behavioural characteristic is extraordinary resistance to environmental feedback.

The scenery changes.

The prices change.

The employment model changes.

The climate changes.

The machine continues speaking.

“WORK HARD.”

Whirr.

“BUY A HOUSE.”

Clunk.

“STOP COMPLAINING.”

Servo motor rotates head 17 degrees.

“NOBODY WANTS TO WORK ANYMORE.”

