2. Methodology

The methodology employed is straightforward.

First, take things influential adults actually say.

Second, place them next to things that are actually happening.

Third, resist the apparently overwhelming institutional temptation to insert six paragraphs explaining that these two pieces of information must never under any circumstances be allowed to meet.

The principal variables examined are:

1. housing affordability;
2. employment expectations under automation;
3. political interpretations of psychological distress;
4. environmental inheritance; and
5. the continued expectation that young adults respond to all four with traditional levels of economic optimism.

Attributed quotations in this paper are real.

The animatronic is metaphorical.

Unfortunately, the contradictions are not.

⸻

3. Housing: Have You Considered Being Born Earlier?

Housing provides perhaps the cleanest example because houses are notoriously resistant to motivational speeches.

In 1997, according to the UK Office for National Statistics, 88% of local-authority areas in England and Wales contained homes selling for less than five times average full-time earnings.

By 2025, that figure was 7%. (Office for National Statistics)

This is an interesting numerical development.

A system in which 88% of areas satisfied a particular affordability threshold became one in which 7% did.

The cultural instruction, however, remained almost perfectly preserved.

Save up and buy somewhere.

In England in 2025, the median home cost £300,000 while median annual full-time earnings were £39,300: an affordability ratio of 7.6. (Office for National Statistics)

The young adult is therefore asked to solve the following engineering problem:

[
\text{House} = 7.6 \times \text{annual gross income}
]

while simultaneously paying rent, tax, energy, transport, food and every other cost associated with maintaining the biological machinery required to continue earning the income.

The recommended intervention is frequently budgeting.

Budgeting is excellent.

Budgeting can determine where money is going.

It cannot make £300,000 equal £150,000.

This distinction deserves greater recognition within economics.

3.1 The Hull Stress Test

Kingston upon Hull provides a particularly useful case because Hull is not generally considered a secret enclave of international billionaires.

In 2025, Hull and Hyndburn were jointly the most affordable local authorities measured by the ONS.

Their affordability ratio was still 4.1 times annual full-time earnings. (Office for National Statistics)

By June 2026, the average Hull house price was approximately £134,000, with first-time buyers paying an average £127,000. (Office for National Statistics)

That is the cheap end of the national experiment.

The author’s family experience provides the more visceral scale.

A house purchased by his late stepfather for approximately £42,000 is today worth roughly £400,000.

The exact property is not intended as a national statistical sample.

It is something more immediately useful:

the same fucking building.

The bricks did not acquire a master’s degree.

The staircase did not develop artificial intelligence.

The kitchen did not discover oil.

It is the same finite physical object embedded within a radically altered housing market.

Yet cultural advice frequently treats the difference between acquiring it then and acquiring it now as a difference in personal discipline.

Thus we arrive at the first law of Animatronic Economics:

Animatronic Law I

When the price of an essential asset increases much faster than the plausibility of obtaining it, attribute the resulting exclusion to the personality of the excluded.

Recommended corrective actions include:

* cancelling Netflix;
* reducing takeaway coffee expenditure;
* working harder;
* acquiring a mysterious “side hustle”;
* and, where possible, selecting wealthier parents.

The last intervention demonstrates particularly strong outcomes.

