4. Employment: Please Train for the Profession We Are Attempting to Delete

Housing alone might produce some anxiety.

Fortunately, the technology sector has arrived to reassure young people about employment.

In 2023, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman was asked what the arrival of increasingly capable artificial intelligence might mean for workers.

His response included:

“Jobs are definitely going to go away, full stop.” (The Atlantic)

In 2025, Altman wrote that there would be difficult consequences including:

“whole classes of jobs going away”. (Sam Altman)

Elon Musk has offered similarly comforting careers advice.

At VivaTech in May 2024 he described a benign AI future in which:

“probably none of us will have a job.” (euronews)

He suggested that people who still wished to work might eventually undertake employment rather like a hobby.

Excellent.

Now imagine being nineteen.

You ask the adults what you should do with your life.

ADULT ONE: Get a proper career.

YOUNG PERSON: Which one?

TECH INDUSTRY: Difficult to say. Whole classes of jobs may disappear.

YOUNG PERSON: Should I still spend years training for one?

ADULT ONE: Obviously.

YOUNG PERSON: Will it exist afterwards?

TECH INDUSTRY: 🤷

ADULT TWO: Your generation lacks commitment.

This produces what we shall call the Motivational Replacement Paradox.

The paradox occurs when a society demands long-term occupational commitment from young people while its most celebrated technological leaders publicly speculate about making enormous sections of that occupation system unnecessary.

The proposition need not even come true.

That is what makes the messaging so extraordinary.

Whether AI eventually replaces 5%, 25% or 80% of current employment is irrelevant to the immediate psychological contradiction.

The message being delivered is:

MESSAGE A

Build a professional identity.
Commit.
Specialise.
Acquire experience.
Demonstrate loyalty.
Take responsibility for your own economic security.

followed immediately by:

MESSAGE B

We are spending hundreds of billions of dollars developing machines intended to perform progressively more of the economically valuable tasks human beings currently perform.

followed by:

MESSAGE C

Why don’t these fucking kids believe in careers anymore?

The animatronic detects no contradiction.

Its firmware predates the update.

