5. The Golden Age

There is a particularly elegant feature of modern technological rhetoric in which the elimination of existing economic structures is frequently presented simultaneously as a threat and a magnificent opportunity.

Altman has also told young people that they are entering what he described as the:

“greatest golden age” of human possibility. (The Atlantic)

This may eventually prove correct.

But consider the informational package received by a young adult:

You are entering history’s greatest golden age.

Your future occupation might disappear.

You must compete with increasingly capable machines.

Housing requires multiples of earnings that would have seemed exceptional to earlier generations.

You must continually retrain.

The climate is warming.

Do not worry.

Remain adaptable.

The civilisation has helpfully replaced security with adaptability.

Adaptability is an excellent human characteristic.

It is also considerably cheaper for institutions than providing security.

A worker with security can make demands.

A worker being permanently “upskilled” is usually too busy updating LinkedIn.

