9. Nobody Wants to Work Anymore

The phrase deserves particular attention.

“Nobody wants to work anymore” is usually spoken as though previous generations awoke each morning sexually aroused by the prospect of industrial labour.

They did not.

People generally wanted what work provided.

Income.

Security.

A home.

Progression.

Status.

Family stability.

A pension.

A reasonable expectation that persistent effort would improve material circumstances.

Work was the mechanism.

The reward mattered.

If the relationship between effort and reward weakens, motivation should be expected to change.

This does not require a new species of defective human.

It requires arithmetic.

Suppose Generation A perceives:

[
10\ \text{years effort} \rightarrow \text{house + security}
]

while Generation B perceives:

[
10\ \text{years effort} \rightarrow \text{rent + uncertain employment + please retrain for AI}
]

and Generation B consequently exhibits lower enthusiasm.

An animatronic concludes:

[
B = \text{lazy}
]

A functioning observer might instead examine the transfer function.

