10. Intergenerational Responsibility Without Intergenerational Theatre

None of this requires pretending that every older person is wealthy.

That would be stupid.

A sixty-year-old struggling to pay rent did not secretly engineer the housing market.

A forty-year-old warehouse worker did not personally design global capitalism.

A retired factory worker did not individually emit two trillion tonnes of carbon.

The meaningful division is not:

[
\text{old people} ; \text{versus} ; \text{young people}
]

It is:

[
\text{institutional power}
; \text{versus} ;
\text{people entering conditions shaped before they arrived}
]

But those of us old enough to have participated in the previous system should at least possess the decency to recognise what is being handed over.

I belong to one of those adult generations.

This is therefore not a teenager screaming at his parents.

It is an adult looking around the room and asking other adults what the fuck we think we’re doing.

We cannot spend decades voting, consuming, building, borrowing, deregulating, regulating, selling, privatising, speculating, automating and generally rearranging the economic furniture and then turn around when the next group enters the room and say:

What have you lot done to the place?

They just fucking got here.

