12. Discussion: What Exactly Do We Want From Them?

This brings us to the central question.

What exactly does the existing adult establishment want from an eighteen-year-old?

We want them ambitious.

Toward jobs we tell them may disappear.

We want them financially responsible.

Inside a housing system dramatically less affordable than it once was.

We want them environmentally responsible.

For accumulated damage overwhelmingly predating their adulthood.

We want them psychologically resilient.

While influential adults publicly describe enormous social and technological upheaval as imminent.

We want them to trust institutions.

While those institutions routinely explain structural outcomes as failures of individual behaviour.

We want them to plan forty years ahead.

The people developing the most economically disruptive technology in generations struggle to tell them what employment will look like in ten.

And when the resulting human being looks at this magnificent package and says:

“This seems a bit shit.”

our response is:

ATTITUDE PROBLEM DETECTED.

Perhaps the surprising result is not that some young adults are disengaged.

Perhaps the surprising result is how many continue participating.

They work.

They study.

They build relationships.

They start companies.

They raise children.

They care for relatives.

They invent things.

They save what they can.

They attempt to construct lives inside conditions they did not select.

And standing beside them is an elderly cultural machine whose rubber face is beginning to perish around the eyes.

Its internal cassette clicks.

Its head turns.

Its mouth opens.

“WHEN I WAS YOUR AGE—”

Nobody has asked.

The machine continues.

