13. Conclusion

Young adults have not mysteriously arrived from another planet with defective economic instincts.

They are human beings responding to incentives.

If home ownership becomes harder, fewer will confidently plan around owning homes.

If career stability becomes uncertain, fewer will emotionally invest in the idea of lifelong careers.

If society’s most influential technologists repeatedly announce coming labour disruption, young workers may take them seriously.

If climatic and economic risks are continually discussed as major civilisational problems, young people may experience concern about them.

These behaviours require no generational pathology.

They require ears.

The defining absurdity of contemporary generational discourse is therefore not that young people refuse to understand the adult world.

It may be that they understand it rather well.

The animatronic does not.

It continues repeating instructions written for another set of prices, another labour market, another housing market and another century.

Work hard.

Buy a house.

Choose a career.

Don’t worry about automation.

Adapt to automation.

Save more.

Consume less.

Grow the economy.

Reduce emissions.

Look after your mental health.

Stop acting like a victim.

Whirr.

Clunk.

Smile.

Repeat.

And perhaps, before commissioning another report into why eighteen-year-olds lack aspiration, the adults running the experiment might finally consider the obvious possibility:

We removed the ladder, automated the lift, sold the building, raised the rent on the pavement outside and then started shouting down from the penthouse asking why the kids aren’t climbing.

